diff --git a/context/design.md b/context/design.md
index 89b1889..24ecb54 100644
--- a/context/design.md
+++ b/context/design.md
@@ -158,6 +158,8 @@ Readability and writability each retain advisory readiness and a notification ge
 
 Independent `async-io` socket fallback operations also attempt immediate progress first. After `WouldBlock`, they await descriptor readiness before another attempt. Repeated polling because a containing task wakes for another descriptor must not retry the blocked syscall. This matters for the io\_uring selector's fallback listener, which can share one supervisor task with active connections.
 
+On Linux, `ReadinessSelector::with_tcp_read_hints()` and `IoUringSelector::with_tcp_read_hints()` explicitly opt borrowed scalar stream readers into socket-wide `TCP_INQ` ancillary messages. Reader construction tries to enable the option; unsupported sockets retain ordinary receives. A synchronous nonblocking `recvmsg` can clear its observed readiness generation only when an intact kernel hint reports zero queued bytes. Missing or truncated hints retain readiness, and positive hints preserve queued data and FIN. Newer notifications survive the generation-aware clear. This avoids treating a short read at an urgent-data boundary as proof of exhaustion. Worker-owned readiness is required for the readiness selector's hint optimization; io\_uring uses its existing persistent poll monitor. Owned completion transfers and message receives retain their existing paths. Other message-oriented APIs and external aliases on an opted-in socket can observe the added control message or control truncation. The option is disabled by default, and applications must measure its ancillary-processing cost against the avoided `WouldBlock` receive.
+
 Bound borrowed stream writers attempt ready progress without a registration-table lookup. Their first `WouldBlock` acquires and retains the shared registration, retries across registration and then clears only the generation observed before a blocked send. Subsequent blocked operations reuse that registration. After writer/output-lane and operation admission checks, a successful send returns before constructing cancellation or readiness wait futures. Immediate errors and short counts also return directly. Cancellation and closure retain the same contract; owned output submission remains separately managed.
 
 `base-scheduler-timers` is extracted as runtime-independent support with no dependency on the scheduler interface. It retains batched queues, stable slots, constant-time cancellation, optional sharded assistance and deadline notifications, and the optional bounded producer driver. Its `local` feature exposes the same queue engine with exclusive ownership and no synchronization or reference counting.
